(Bug #86, the other half: a buffer sink must not merely honour a
 precision, it must honour the SAME one Print does. The fix redirects
 the single precision printer at the destination buffer rather than
 writing a second one, so everything that printer knows has to arrive
 with it - the exact decimal expansion, round-half-to-even on a tie,
 the carry that lengthens the integer part, and the magnitudes at or
 beyond 2^63 that #60 was about.

 Each value is rendered twice, by Print and through a buffer. The two
 lines of every pair are identical by construction; a second, drifting
 renderer is exactly what makes them differ.)

a float called 'pi' is 3.14159.
Print "pi to 2:  {pi:.2}".
Create a buffer called two.
copy "pi to 2:  {pi:.2}" to two.
Print two.

Print "pi to 20: {pi:.20}".
Create a buffer called twenty.
copy "pi to 20: {pi:.20}" to twenty.
Print twenty.

Print "pi to 50: {pi:.50}".
Create a buffer called fifty.
copy "pi to 50: {pi:.50}" to fifty.
Print fifty.

(No places asked for, so no point is printed - in either sink.)
Print "pi to 0:  {pi:.0}".
Create a buffer called none.
copy "pi to 0:  {pi:.0}" to none.
Print none.

(An exact half goes to the even digit, which is what printf does.)
a float called 'two and a half' is 2.5.
Print "2.5 to 0: {two and a half:.0}".
Create a buffer called tie.
copy "2.5 to 0: {two and a half:.0}" to tie.
Print tie.

(A carry out of the leading digit lengthens the integer part.)
a float called 'nearly ten' is 9.9999.
Print "9.9999 to 3: {nearly ten:.3}".
Create a buffer called carried.
copy "9.9999 to 3: {nearly ten:.3}" to carried.
Print carried.

(A negative keeps its sign and rounds by magnitude.)
a float called 'owed' is -1.25.
Print "-1.25 to 1: {owed:.1}".
Create a buffer called signed.
copy "-1.25 to 1: {owed:.1}" to signed.
Print signed.

(Past 2^63 the digits come from the big-integer routine, not from a
 register conversion - the sink must reach that arm too.)
a float called 'ten sextillion' is 10000000000000000000000.0.
Print "10000000000000000000000 to 3: {ten sextillion:.3}".
Create a buffer called enormous.
copy "10000000000000000000000 to 3: {ten sextillion:.3}" to enormous.
Print enormous.

(Every place of the exact expansion, then the zeros that follow it -
 the value has ended, it has not run out of accuracy.)
a float called 'a tenth' is 0.1.
Print "0.1 to 60: {a tenth:.60}".
Create a buffer called exact.
copy "0.1 to 60: {a tenth:.60}" to exact.
Print exact.

(400 places outgrows a fresh buffer several times over, so the
 destination is reallocated part-way through the render and the sink
 has to follow the moved pointer rather than keep writing to the old
 one.)
Print "0.1 to 400: {a tenth:.400}".
Create a buffer called reallocated.
copy "0.1 to 400: {a tenth:.400}" to reallocated.
Print reallocated.
